export const A6={title:'Respiratory disorders and sleep apnea',hook:'Cough and shortness of breath are among the most common reasons people see a clinician. The cause may be a simple virus, asthma, COPD, pneumonia, tuberculosis, a blood clot or lung cancer—and loud snoring with daytime sleepiness may point to obstructive sleep apnea. Respiratory disorders are 6% of the test.',objectives:['Distinguish asthma and COPD using history and spirometry.','Diagnose and treat community-acquired pneumonia and assess severity.','Describe latent and active tuberculosis and lung cancer warning signs.','Recognize obstructive sleep apnea and its treatments, including CPAP and UPPP.'],sections:[
['Asthma and COPD',[
'**Asthma** is chronic airway inflammation with **reversible** narrowing. Patients—often younger, with allergies or eczema—have episodes of **wheezing, cough (often at night), chest tightness and shortness of breath** triggered by allergens, exercise, cold air, smoke or infections. **Spirometry** shows obstruction that improves by at least **12%** after a bronchodilator. Treatment uses an **inhaled corticosteroid** controller and a quick-relief inhaler, with stepwise increases; a written **asthma action plan** guides home care.',
'**COPD** (emphysema and chronic bronchitis) is usually caused by **smoking** and shows **persistent, not fully reversible** obstruction: a post-bronchodilator **FEV₁/FVC below 0.70**. Patients over 40 have progressive breathlessness, chronic productive cough and frequent exacerbations. Treatment includes **smoking cessation** (the most important step), long-acting bronchodilators (LAMA, LABA), inhaled steroids for frequent exacerbations, **pulmonary rehabilitation**, vaccines and oxygen for low saturation. Exacerbations are treated with bronchodilators, oral steroids and antibiotics when sputum changes.',
sort('Asthma or COPD?',['More typical of asthma','More typical of COPD'],[['Onset in childhood with allergies',0],['40-pack-year smoker over 50',1],['Obstruction fully reverses after albuterol',0],['FEV₁/FVC stays below 0.70 after bronchodilator',1],['Barrel chest and chronic productive cough',1],['Night cough triggered by cold air',0]]),
label('resp','The respiratory system',[['nasal','Nasal cavity','Warms and filters air.'],['pharynx','Pharynx','Collapses during sleep in obstructive sleep apnea.'],['trachea','Trachea','Main airway.'],['bronchi','Bronchi','Inflamed and narrowed in asthma.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Obstructed in COPD.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Destroyed in emphysema; filled in pneumonia.']])]],
['Pneumonia and tuberculosis',[
'**Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)** causes **fever, cough with sputum, pleuritic chest pain and shortness of breath**, with **crackles** or dullness on exam. A **chest X-ray** shows an infiltrate. *Streptococcus pneumoniae* is the most common bacterial cause; viruses also cause pneumonia. Severity tools such as **CURB-65** (Confusion, Urea, Respiratory rate ≥30, Blood pressure low, age ≥65) help decide outpatient versus hospital care. Treatment is **antibiotics** chosen by setting and risk, fluids, rest and follow-up; prevention includes pneumococcal and influenza vaccines.',
'**Tuberculosis (TB)** is caused by *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* and spreads through the **air**. **Latent TB** has no symptoms and is not contagious but can become active; it is found by a skin test or **interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA)** and treated to prevent disease. **Active TB** causes **chronic cough (3+ weeks), hemoptysis, fever, night sweats and weight loss**; it requires **airborne isolation**, sputum testing and multi-drug therapy for months (often starting with rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol).',
match('Respiratory infections',[['Lobar infiltrate on X-ray with fever','Pneumonia'],['CURB-65','Pneumonia severity score'],['IGRA','Blood test for TB infection'],['Night sweats, hemoptysis, weight loss','Active tuberculosis'],['Airborne isolation','Required for active TB'],['Pneumococcal vaccine','Prevents pneumonia']])]],
['Lung cancer and pulmonary embolism',[
'**Lung cancer** is the leading cause of cancer death; **smoking** causes most cases, and radon and asbestos add risk. Warning signs include a **persistent cough**, **hemoptysis**, weight loss, hoarseness, chest pain and recurrent pneumonia. Diagnosis requires imaging (CT) and **biopsy**; types are **non-small cell** (most common) and **small cell** (aggressive, linked to smoking). Annual **low-dose CT** screening is recommended for eligible adults 50–80 with a 20 pack-year history.',
"Respiratory treatment plans in case files commonly include oxygen to a target saturation, bronchodilators, steroids or antibiotics as indicated, vaccination, smoking cessation counseling, inhaler-technique teaching and follow-up imaging or spirometry.",
'**Pulmonary embolism** (covered with circulatory disorders) must stay on the differential for sudden shortness of breath with a fast heart rate, especially after surgery, travel or estrogen use. **Pleural effusion** (fluid around the lung) causes dullness to percussion and decreased breath sounds, and **pneumothorax** (air around the lung) causes sudden pleuritic pain and absent breath sounds on one side, sometimes after trauma or in tall, thin young men.',
sort('Match the finding',['Pneumothorax','Pleural effusion','Pneumonia'],[['Absent breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side',0],['Dullness and decreased breath sounds at the base',1],['Fever, crackles and a lobar infiltrate',2],['Sudden pleuritic pain in a tall young man',0],['Fluid layering on the chest X-ray',1],['Productive cough and egophony',2]])]],
['Obstructive sleep apnea',[
'**Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)** occurs when the upper airway—the soft palate, uvula, tongue and pharynx—**collapses during sleep**, stopping or reducing breathing many times an hour. Symptoms include **loud snoring**, witnessed **pauses in breathing**, gasping, **daytime sleepiness**, morning headaches and poor concentration. Risk factors include **obesity**, a large neck, male sex, older age and enlarged tonsils (in children). The **STOP-BANG** questionnaire screens for OSA, and **polysomnography** (a sleep study) diagnoses it by measuring the apnea-hypopnea index.',
'Untreated OSA raises the risk of hypertension, heart disease, stroke, atrial fibrillation, diabetes and car crashes. First-line treatment is **CPAP** (continuous positive airway pressure), which keeps the airway open. Other options include **weight loss**, positional therapy, oral appliances, avoiding alcohol and sedatives, hypoglossal nerve stimulation and surgery such as **uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP)**, which removes excess tissue from the throat. Children with OSA from large tonsils often have a **tonsillectomy**.',
flip('Sleep apnea facts',[['Mechanism','Upper airway collapses during sleep'],['Classic symptoms','Loud snoring, pauses, daytime sleepiness'],['Screening','STOP-BANG questionnaire'],['Diagnosis','Polysomnography (sleep study)'],['First-line treatment','CPAP'],['Surgical option','Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP)']])]],
['Practice case: cough and fever',[
kase('Case: cough and fever',{a:{text:'A 72-year-old woman has 3 days of fever, productive cough and right-sided pleuritic pain. Temp 102.1 °F, RR 24, BP 104/62, SpO₂ 91%. She is mildly confused. Crackles are heard at the right base. (Case invented for practice.) Which test confirms the leading diagnosis?',choices:[['Chest X-ray','b','Correct. An infiltrate confirms pneumonia.',true],['Spirometry','b','Spirometry is for asthma or COPD.',false]]},b:{text:'The X-ray shows right lower-lobe consolidation. Her CURB-65 score counts confusion and age 65 or older; her respiratory rate and blood pressure do not meet the cutoffs. How should she be managed?',choices:[['Admit for IV antibiotics, oxygen and monitoring','c','Correct. Confusion, age and low oxygen support hospital care.',true],['Send home with no follow-up','c','She has several severity features.',false]]},c:{text:'Plan: blood and sputum cultures, IV antibiotics, oxygen, fluids, follow-up X-ray as needed, and pneumococcal and influenza vaccination before discharge.',end:true}})]],
["Upper respiratory and viral infections",["Most **upper respiratory infections** (colds) and acute **bronchitis** are viral and do not need antibiotics; treatment is rest, fluids and symptom relief. **Influenza** causes sudden fever, body aches, cough and fatigue; **oseltamivir** helps most when started within 48 hours, especially in high-risk patients, and annual vaccination prevents it. **COVID-19** ranges from mild illness to pneumonia; testing, antivirals for high-risk patients and vaccination are key. **Acute sinusitis** is usually viral; bacterial sinusitis is suspected when symptoms last more than 10 days or worsen after improving.","**Pharyngitis** is also usually viral. **Strep throat** is more likely with fever, swollen tender neck nodes, tonsillar exudate and **no cough**; the **Centor criteria** guide rapid strep testing, and confirmed cases are treated with penicillin or amoxicillin to prevent rheumatic fever. Practicing antibiotic stewardship—treating only bacterial infections—is part of a high-quality treatment plan.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Antibiotics usually indicated?","buckets":["Usually yes","Usually no"],"items":[["Positive rapid strep test",0],["Common cold",1],["Acute viral bronchitis",1],["Community-acquired pneumonia",0],["Three days of runny nose and congestion",1],["Pyelonephritis",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Asthma is reversible obstruction (≥12% bronchodilator response; ICS controller); COPD is persistent obstruction (FEV₁/FVC <0.70) from smoking, treated with cessation, LAMA/LABA and rehab.','Pneumonia: fever, productive cough, crackles, infiltrate on X-ray; CURB-65 guides admission; TB is airborne—latent (IGRA/skin test) versus active (cough, night sweats, weight loss).','OSA: airway collapse during sleep with snoring and sleepiness; polysomnography; CPAP first-line; UPPP and other surgery for selected patients.'],terms:[['Spirometry','A breathing test that measures airflow.'],['Polysomnography','An overnight sleep study.'],['Consolidation','Lung tissue filled with fluid or pus, as in pneumonia.']],mcqs:[q('A patient might have a uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP) to treat what condition?','Sleep apnea','Impacted molar','Cavity','Canker sores','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which spirometry finding defines COPD?','Post-bronchodilator FEV₁/FVC below 0.70','Normal FEV₁','FEV₁/FVC above 0.90','Full reversal with albuterol','Persistent obstruction.'),q('What is first-line treatment for obstructive sleep apnea?','CPAP','Antibiotics','Inhaled corticosteroids','Chemotherapy','Keeps the airway open.'),q('Which test diagnoses sleep apnea?','Polysomnography','Chest X-ray','Lipid panel','Colonoscopy','A sleep study.'),q('The most common bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia is:','Streptococcus pneumoniae','E. coli','Staphylococcus epidermidis','Mycobacterium tuberculosis','Pneumococcus.'),q('Which isolation is required for active TB?','Airborne','Contact','Droplet','None','N95 and a negative-pressure room.'),q('Absent breath sounds on one side after sudden pain suggest:','Pneumothorax','Asthma','Bronchitis','Normal lungs','Air around the lung.'),q('The most important treatment for COPD is:','Smoking cessation','Antibiotics daily','Bed rest','A low-protein diet','Slows decline.')],frq:{prompt:'A 48-year-old man with obesity reports loud snoring, morning headaches and falling asleep while driving. His wife has seen him stop breathing at night, and his blood pressure is 152/96. Present his diagnosis, how it would be confirmed and a complete treatment plan.',rubric:[['Diagnosis and evidence',['obstructive sleep apnea','snoring','pauses','daytime sleepiness','obesity','hypertension']],['Confirmation',['stop-bang','polysomnography','sleep study','apnea-hypopnea']],['Treatment',['cpap','weight loss','positional','oral appliance','alcohol','sedatives','uppp','surgery']],['Risks and follow-up',['driving','hypertension','heart','stroke','follow-up','adherence','education']]],model:'The most likely diagnosis is obstructive sleep apnea. He has loud snoring, witnessed pauses in breathing, morning headaches and dangerous daytime sleepiness, along with obesity and hypertension, which is both a risk factor and a consequence. I would screen with the STOP-BANG questionnaire and confirm with polysomnography, either in a sleep lab or at home, measuring the apnea-hypopnea index to grade severity. First-line treatment is CPAP, which keeps the upper airway open; we would fit a mask, educate him on nightly use and check adherence at follow-up. Additional measures include weight loss through diet and exercise, avoiding alcohol and sedatives before bed, sleeping on his side and treating nasal congestion. If he cannot tolerate CPAP, options include an oral appliance, hypoglossal nerve stimulation or surgery such as uvulopalatopharyngoplasty. Because untreated OSA raises the risk of crashes, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, heart disease and stroke, I would counsel him not to drive while drowsy, treat his blood pressure and recheck it once his sleep apnea is treated.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_ASTHMA,MEDLINE_COPD,MEDLINE_PNA,CDC_TB,MEDLINE_UPPP]};

export const A7={title:'Digestive disorders',hook:'Abdominal pain is one of the most common—and most challenging—complaints in medicine. The location, timing and associated symptoms point to very different organs: appendix, gallbladder, pancreas, stomach, liver or colon. Digestive disorders are 10% of the test, and abdominal cases are favorites for case challenges because the clinical course unfolds step by step.',objectives:['Use pain location and features to build an abdominal differential.','Diagnose and plan treatment for appendicitis, cholecystitis and pancreatitis.','Compare GERD, peptic ulcer disease, Crohn’s disease, ulcerative colitis, celiac disease and IBS.','Describe viral hepatitis, cirrhosis, GI bleeding and colorectal cancer.'],sections:[
['Abdominal pain by location',[
'Think about the organs beneath each region. **Right upper quadrant (RUQ):** gallbladder (cholecystitis), liver (hepatitis), duodenum. **Epigastric:** stomach (gastritis, ulcer), pancreas (pancreatitis) and sometimes the heart. **Left upper quadrant:** spleen, stomach. **Periumbilical:** early appendicitis, small bowel. **Right lower quadrant (RLQ):** appendix, ovary (ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion), cecum. **Left lower quadrant (LLQ):** sigmoid colon (**diverticulitis**), ovary. **Suprapubic:** bladder, uterus.',
'Red flags include **peritoneal signs** (rigidity, rebound tenderness, guarding), signs of shock, **GI bleeding**, fever with jaundice, a pulsating abdominal mass (possible aortic aneurysm) and pain out of proportion to the exam (possible bowel ischemia). Always consider **pregnancy** in people who can become pregnant, because ectopic pregnancy is a can’t-miss diagnosis.',
sort('Which quadrant is most typical?',['Right upper','Right lower','Left lower','Epigastric'],[['Cholecystitis',0],['Appendicitis (after migration)',1],['Diverticulitis',2],['Pancreatitis',3],['Hepatitis',0],['Peptic ulcer',3]]),
label('gi','The digestive system',[['liver','Liver','Inflamed in hepatitis; scarred in cirrhosis.'],['gallbladder','Gallbladder','Stores bile; stones cause cholecystitis.'],['pancreas','Pancreas','Inflamed in pancreatitis.'],['stomach','Stomach','Ulcers from H. pylori or NSAIDs.'],['appendix','Appendix','Inflamed in appendicitis.'],['largeint','Large intestine','Diverticulitis, ulcerative colitis and colorectal cancer.']])]],
['Appendicitis, cholecystitis and pancreatitis',[
'**Appendicitis** classically begins with **periumbilical pain** that **migrates to the RLQ** (McBurney’s point), with **anorexia**, nausea, low-grade fever and an elevated white count. **CT** (or ultrasound in children and pregnancy) confirms it. Treatment is **appendectomy** (often laparoscopic) with antibiotics; perforation causes peritonitis. **Cholecystitis** usually comes from a **gallstone** blocking the cystic duct: **RUQ pain** after fatty meals, radiating to the right shoulder, fever and a positive **Murphy’s sign**; **ultrasound** shows stones and wall thickening. Treatment includes NPO, IV fluids, antibiotics and **cholecystectomy**.',
'**Acute pancreatitis** causes severe **epigastric pain radiating to the back**, nausea and vomiting. Diagnosis requires two of three: typical pain, **lipase (or amylase) at least 3 times normal** and imaging findings. The most common causes are **gallstones** and **alcohol**; others include high triglycerides and certain drugs. Treatment is supportive—aggressive **IV fluids**, pain control, early feeding when tolerated—plus treating the cause (cholecystectomy for gallstones, alcohol cessation).',
match('Classic features',[['Pain migrating from navel to RLQ','Appendicitis'],['Positive Murphy’s sign','Cholecystitis'],['Epigastric pain radiating to the back','Pancreatitis'],['Lipase over 3× normal','Pancreatitis'],['Ultrasound shows gallstones and thick wall','Cholecystitis'],['Treatment: appendectomy','Appendicitis']])]],
['Stomach and bowel disorders',[
'**GERD** causes heartburn and regurgitation, worse lying down or after large meals; treatment includes weight loss, raising the head of the bed, avoiding late meals and triggers, and **proton pump inhibitors**. Alarm features (trouble swallowing, weight loss, bleeding) require **endoscopy**. **Peptic ulcer disease** is caused mainly by ***Helicobacter pylori*** and **NSAIDs**; it causes burning epigastric pain and can bleed or perforate. Treatment: PPIs, stopping NSAIDs and **eradicating *H. pylori*** with antibiotic combinations.',
'**Crohn’s disease** can affect any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus, with **skip lesions** and **transmural** inflammation that causes fistulas and strictures; it often involves the terminal ileum. **Ulcerative colitis** affects only the **colon**, starting at the **rectum** and spreading **continuously**, with **bloody diarrhea**. Both are treated with anti-inflammatory and immune-modifying drugs and sometimes surgery. **Celiac disease** is an immune reaction to **gluten**, diagnosed with **tTG-IgA** antibodies and small-bowel biopsy and treated with a strict gluten-free diet. **Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)** causes recurrent pain with altered bowel habits without structural disease. **Diverticulitis** is inflammation of colon pouches, usually LLQ pain with fever in older adults.',
sort('Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis?',['Crohn’s disease','Ulcerative colitis'],[['Mouth-to-anus involvement',0],['Starts at the rectum and spreads continuously',1],['Skip lesions',0],['Transmural inflammation and fistulas',0],['Limited to the colon',1],['Bloody diarrhea is typical',1]])]],
['Liver disease and GI bleeding',[
'**Viral hepatitis**: **hepatitis A** spreads by the fecal-oral route and is usually short-lived; **hepatitis B** spreads through blood, sex and birth and can become chronic—prevented by vaccine; **hepatitis C** spreads mainly through blood (shared needles) and is now **curable** with direct-acting antivirals. Symptoms include fatigue, nausea, **jaundice**, dark urine and RUQ discomfort; ALT and AST are elevated. **Cirrhosis**—scarring from chronic hepatitis, alcohol or fatty liver disease—causes **ascites**, **jaundice**, easy bruising, **varices** and confusion (**hepatic encephalopathy**), and raises the risk of liver cancer.',
'**Upper GI bleeding** (above the ligament of Treitz—esophagus, stomach, duodenum) presents with **hematemesis** (vomiting blood or “coffee grounds”) or **melena** (black, tarry stool); common causes are ulcers and varices. **Lower GI bleeding** often presents with **hematochezia** (bright red blood) from diverticulosis, hemorrhoids, colitis or cancer. Unstable patients need two large IV lines, fluids, blood transfusion and urgent endoscopy or colonoscopy.',
match('Liver and bleeding terms',[['Hepatitis A','Fecal-oral spread; vaccine available'],['Hepatitis B','Blood, sexual and birth spread; vaccine available'],['Hepatitis C','Blood-borne; curable with antivirals'],['Ascites','Fluid in the abdomen in cirrhosis'],['Melena','Black, tarry stool from upper GI bleeding'],['Hematochezia','Bright red blood from the rectum']])]],
['Colorectal cancer',[
'**Colorectal cancer** usually develops from **adenomatous polyps** over years, which is why screening (starting at **45**) works—polyps can be removed before they become cancer. Risk factors include age, family history, inflammatory bowel disease, obesity, smoking, alcohol, red and processed meat and inherited syndromes such as Lynch syndrome. Symptoms include a **change in bowel habits**, **rectal bleeding**, **iron-deficiency anemia** (especially in an older adult), abdominal pain and weight loss.',
'Diagnosis uses **colonoscopy with biopsy**, then CT staging and the tumor marker **CEA**. Treatment depends on stage: **surgical resection** for most, **chemotherapy** for node-positive (stage III) disease, and radiation for some rectal cancers. A new iron-deficiency anemia in a man or postmenopausal woman should prompt evaluation for GI blood loss.',
"For every GI case, the plan should address hydration and nutrition, pain and nausea control, the specific cause (surgery, antibiotics, acid suppression or diet), monitoring for complications such as perforation, bleeding or obstruction, and prevention—vaccines for hepatitis A and B, alcohol reduction, weight management and age-appropriate cancer screening.",
chk('A 66-year-old man has new iron-deficiency anemia and fatigue. What evaluation is most important?',['Colonoscopy and upper endoscopy to look for GI blood loss','Iron pills only, no further testing','A sleep study','Spirometry'],'Occult GI bleeding, including colorectal cancer, must be ruled out.')]],
['Practice case: abdominal pain',[
kase('Case: abdominal pain',{a:{text:'A 19-year-old man had periumbilical pain last night that moved to the right lower quadrant. He has anorexia and nausea. Temp 100.6 °F, WBC 14,200, tenderness at McBurney’s point with rebound. (Case invented for practice.) What is the leading diagnosis?',choices:[['Acute appendicitis','b','Correct. Classic migration, anorexia, fever, leukocytosis and peritoneal signs.',true],['Gastroenteritis','b','Peritoneal signs and RLQ localization favor appendicitis.',false]]},b:{text:'Which test confirms it in this adult?',choices:[['CT abdomen and pelvis','c','Correct. CT is the usual adult test.',true],['Colonoscopy','c','Colonoscopy is not used for suspected appendicitis.',false]]},c:{text:'Plan: NPO, IV fluids, pain control, IV antibiotics and laparoscopic appendectomy, then wound care, activity guidance and follow-up.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Map pain to organs: RUQ gallbladder/liver, epigastric stomach/pancreas, RLQ appendix/ovary, LLQ sigmoid; consider pregnancy and peritoneal signs.','Appendicitis (migration to RLQ, CT, appendectomy), cholecystitis (fatty meals, Murphy’s sign, ultrasound, cholecystectomy), pancreatitis (epigastric to back, lipase ≥3×, fluids; gallstones and alcohol).','GERD and PUD (H. pylori, NSAIDs, PPIs); Crohn’s (mouth to anus, skip, transmural) versus UC (rectum, continuous, bloody diarrhea); celiac (tTG-IgA); hepatitis A/B/C; melena vs hematochezia; colorectal cancer screening from 45.'],terms:[['Melena','Black, tarry stool from upper GI bleeding.'],['Murphy’s sign','Pain and stopped breathing on deep palpation of the RUQ, suggesting cholecystitis.'],['Skip lesions','Patches of disease separated by normal bowel, typical of Crohn’s.']],mcqs:[q('Pain that starts around the navel and moves to the RLQ suggests:','Appendicitis','Cholecystitis','Pancreatitis','GERD','Classic migration.'),q('The best first imaging for suspected cholecystitis is:','Ultrasound','MRI brain','Spirometry','Colonoscopy','Gallstones and wall thickening.'),q('The two most common causes of acute pancreatitis are:','Gallstones and alcohol','Viruses and bacteria','Aspirin and caffeine','Smoking and stress','Plus triglycerides and drugs.'),q('Which infection is the main cause of peptic ulcers?','Helicobacter pylori','E. coli','Hepatitis A','Candida','Plus NSAIDs.'),q('Ulcerative colitis:','Starts at the rectum and spreads continuously','Affects mouth to anus','Has skip lesions','Is transmural','Colon only.'),q('Celiac disease is triggered by:','Gluten','Lactose','Fructose','Caffeine','Immune reaction.'),q('Which hepatitis is curable with direct-acting antivirals?','Hepatitis C','Hepatitis A','Hepatitis B','All three','DAAs.'),q('Black, tarry stool is called:','Melena','Hematochezia','Hematemesis','Steatorrhea','Upper GI bleeding.')],frq:{prompt:'A 52-year-old woman has severe epigastric pain radiating to her back, vomiting and a lipase five times normal. Ultrasound shows gallstones. Present the clinical course, diagnosis and a complete treatment plan.',rubric:[['Clinical course',['epigastric','back','vomiting','onset','meal','chronological']],['Diagnosis',['acute pancreatitis','gallstone','lipase','3 times','ultrasound']],['Acute treatment',['iv fluids','pain','npo','early feeding','monitor','electrolytes']],['Definitive and preventive care',['cholecystectomy','same admission','ercp','alcohol','triglycerides','education','follow-up']]],model:'The patient developed severe epigastric pain radiating to her back with nausea and vomiting, often after a meal; on arrival she had a lipase five times the upper limit of normal, and ultrasound showed gallstones. The diagnosis is acute gallstone pancreatitis: she meets at least two of the three criteria—characteristic pain and a lipase more than three times normal—and gallstones are the most common cause. Initial treatment is supportive: aggressive intravenous fluids, pain control, antiemetics and close monitoring of vital signs, urine output, electrolytes, glucose and calcium, watching for complications such as organ failure, necrosis or infection. Feeding by mouth starts early once pain improves and she can tolerate it. Because gallstones caused the attack, she should have a cholecystectomy, ideally during the same admission once she recovers, to prevent recurrence; if a stone is blocking the common bile duct or cholangitis develops, ERCP removes it. The plan also includes avoiding alcohol, checking triglycerides, reviewing medications, education about warning signs and follow-up.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_APPY,NIDDK_GALL,NIDDK_IBD,CDC_HEP]};

export const A8={title:'Endocrine, urinary and reproductive disorders',hook:'A teenager with thirst, weight loss and fruity breath. A woman with fatigue, weight gain and feeling cold all the time. A man with sudden colicky flank pain and blood in his urine. A young woman with pelvic pain and a positive pregnancy test. Endocrine (6%), urinary (4%) and reproductive (4%) disorders together make up 14% of the test.',objectives:['Diagnose diabetes, DKA and thyroid and adrenal disorders using lab criteria.','Explain kidney structure and function and diagnose UTI, pyelonephritis, kidney stones, AKI and CKD.','Recognize reproductive emergencies such as ectopic pregnancy and testicular torsion.','Describe common sexually transmitted infections and other reproductive disorders.'],sections:[
['Diabetes',[
'Diabetes is diagnosed by any of: **A1c ≥6.5%**, **fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL**, **2-hour glucose ≥200 mg/dL** on an oral glucose tolerance test, or a **random glucose ≥200 mg/dL with symptoms** (thirst, urination, weight loss). Abnormal results without symptoms are repeated to confirm. **Prediabetes** is an A1c of 5.7–6.4% or fasting glucose of 100–125. **Type 1** diabetes is autoimmune and needs insulin; **type 2** involves insulin resistance and is linked to obesity, inactivity and family history.',
'**Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)**, most often in type 1, causes high glucose, **ketones** and **metabolic acidosis**—with vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration, **fruity breath** and deep, rapid **Kussmaul breathing**. Treatment: **IV fluids**, **insulin infusion**, **potassium replacement** and treating the trigger. Long-term diabetes care includes lifestyle change, metformin and other agents, insulin when needed, blood pressure and cholesterol control, and screening for **eye, kidney, nerve and foot** complications.',
sort('Diagnostic category',['Normal','Prediabetes','Diabetes'],[['A1c 5.2%',0],['A1c 6.1%',1],['A1c 7.0%',2],['Fasting glucose 112 mg/dL',1],['Fasting glucose 131 mg/dL (confirmed)',2],['Fasting glucose 88 mg/dL',0]]),
{t:'w',kind:'glucose',title:'Glucose and insulin',prompt:'Compare a healthy body with type 1 and type 2 diabetes after a meal.'}]],
['Thyroid and adrenal disorders',[
'**Hypothyroidism** causes fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin and slowed reflexes; the most common U.S. cause is **Hashimoto’s thyroiditis**. Labs show **high TSH** and **low free T4**. Treatment is **levothyroxine**, adjusted by TSH. **Hyperthyroidism** causes weight loss, heat intolerance, anxiety, tremor, palpitations and diarrhea; **Graves’ disease** (autoimmune) adds **exophthalmos** (bulging eyes) and goiter. Labs show **low TSH** and **high T4/T3**. Treatment includes beta blockers for symptoms, **methimazole**, radioactive iodine or surgery.',
'**Cushing syndrome** (excess cortisol, often from steroid medicines) causes a round “moon” face, central weight gain, purple **striae**, high glucose and blood pressure, and bone loss. **Addison’s disease** (adrenal insufficiency) causes fatigue, weight loss, low blood pressure, **hyperpigmentation**, low sodium and high potassium; an **adrenal crisis** is an emergency treated with IV hydrocortisone and fluids.',
sort('Hypothyroid or hyperthyroid?',['Hypothyroidism','Hyperthyroidism'],[['High TSH',0],['Low TSH',1],['Cold intolerance and weight gain',0],['Heat intolerance, tremor, weight loss',1],['Exophthalmos',1],['Hashimoto’s thyroiditis',0]]),
label('endocrine','Endocrine glands',[['pituitary','Pituitary','Makes TSH and other control hormones.'],['thyroid','Thyroid','Underactive in Hashimoto’s; overactive in Graves’.'],['adrenal','Adrenal glands','Excess cortisol in Cushing; deficiency in Addison’s.'],['pancreas','Pancreas','Beta cells make insulin.'],['parathyroid','Parathyroid','Controls blood calcium.']])]],
['Kidneys and urinary disorders',[
'Each kidney contains about a million **nephrons**, the filtering units made of a **glomerulus** and tubules. Kidneys filter waste, balance fluids and electrolytes, regulate blood pressure and make hormones. **Urinary tract infection (cystitis)** causes **dysuria**, frequency and urgency; urinalysis shows **leukocyte esterase** and **nitrites**; treatment is a short antibiotic course. **Pyelonephritis** (kidney infection) adds **fever, flank pain**, **costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness** and nausea and needs a longer antibiotic course or hospitalization.',
'**Kidney stones** cause sudden, severe, **colicky flank pain radiating to the groin**, with **hematuria**; **noncontrast CT** confirms them. Treatment: pain control, fluids, medicines to help passage and procedures for large stones. **Acute kidney injury (AKI)** is a rapid rise in creatinine—**prerenal** (dehydration, low blood flow), **intrarenal** (kidney damage from drugs or toxins) or **postrenal** (obstruction such as an enlarged prostate). **Chronic kidney disease (CKD)** is an **eGFR below 60** or kidney damage for **3 months or more**; **diabetes and hypertension** are the leading causes. ACE inhibitors or ARBs and SGLT2 inhibitors slow progression.',
label('urinary','The urinary system',[['kidney','Kidney','Contains about a million nephrons.'],['ureter','Ureter','Kidney stones cause colicky pain here.'],['bladder','Bladder','Inflamed in cystitis.'],['urethra','Urethra','Shorter in females, raising UTI risk.']]),
match('Urinary findings',[['Nitrite-positive urine with dysuria','Cystitis'],['Fever, flank pain, CVA tenderness','Pyelonephritis'],['Colicky flank-to-groin pain with hematuria','Kidney stone'],['Creatinine rises after severe dehydration','Prerenal AKI'],['eGFR below 60 for over 3 months','Chronic kidney disease'],['Filtering unit of the kidney','Nephron']])]],
['Reproductive disorders',[
'**Ectopic pregnancy**—implantation outside the uterus, usually in a fallopian tube—causes **pelvic pain**, **vaginal bleeding** and a **positive hCG** with no intrauterine pregnancy on **transvaginal ultrasound**. Rupture causes internal bleeding and shock; it is an emergency treated with **methotrexate** (if stable and early) or **surgery**. **Testicular torsion** causes **sudden, severe testicular pain**, swelling, nausea and an **absent cremasteric reflex**; it needs **surgery within about 6 hours** to save the testicle.',
'**Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)** causes irregular periods, signs of excess androgen (acne, hair growth) and polycystic ovaries, with links to insulin resistance. **Endometriosis** causes painful periods and infertility. **Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)** causes urinary hesitancy, weak stream and nighttime urination in older men. **Sexually transmitted infections**: **chlamydia** (most commonly reported bacterial STI, often without symptoms) and **gonorrhea** can cause pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility; **syphilis** progresses through stages starting with a painless chancre; **HPV** causes genital warts and cervical cancer (prevented by vaccine); **HIV** attacks CD4 cells and is controlled with antiretroviral therapy. Partners are treated, and testing is confidential.',
"Reproductive and urinary plans should include confidentiality, partner notification for STIs, contraception counseling, vaccination against HPV and hepatitis B, and follow-up testing. For men over 50 with urinary symptoms, consider BPH and discuss prostate cancer screening with shared decision-making.",
sort('Emergency or routine evaluation?',['Emergency','Routine or scheduled'],[['Pelvic pain and positive hCG with no uterine pregnancy on ultrasound',0],['Sudden severe testicular pain with absent cremasteric reflex',0],['Irregular periods and acne over a year',1],['Nighttime urination in a 68-year-old man',1],['Shock with a ruptured ectopic pregnancy',0],['Routine chlamydia screening in a 22-year-old',1]])]],
['Practice case: thirst and weight loss',[
kase('Case: DKA',{a:{text:'A 15-year-old has two weeks of thirst, frequent urination and 8 pounds of weight loss. Today she is vomiting, breathing deeply and fast, with fruity breath. Glucose 486 mg/dL, positive ketones, pH 7.18. (Case invented for practice.) What is the diagnosis?',choices:[['Diabetic ketoacidosis from new type 1 diabetes','b','Correct. Hyperglycemia, ketones and acidosis.',true],['Gastroenteritis','b','Glucose, ketones and acidosis point to DKA.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the immediate treatment?',choices:[['IV fluids, insulin infusion and potassium replacement with close monitoring','c','Correct. Fluids, insulin and electrolytes.',true],['Oral metformin and discharge','c','DKA needs IV treatment.',false]]},c:{text:'Long-term plan: basal-bolus insulin, glucose monitoring, diabetes education for her and her family, sick-day rules, nutrition counseling and endocrinology follow-up.',end:true}})]],
["Fluids and electrolytes",["Many endocrine and kidney cases involve electrolytes. **Hyponatremia** (sodium below 135) can cause confusion and seizures; causes include heart failure, cirrhosis, SIADH, thiazides and excessive water intake, and it must be corrected slowly. **Hyperkalemia** (potassium above 5.0) is common in kidney failure, DKA and with ACE inhibitors; severe levels cause dangerous heart rhythms with **peaked T waves** and need urgent treatment. **Hypokalemia** often follows diuretics or vomiting and causes weakness and arrhythmias.","**Dehydration** raises the BUN-to-creatinine ratio and can cause prerenal kidney injury; it is treated with oral or IV fluids. In case presentations, mention abnormal electrolytes and how you would correct and monitor them—judges look for treatment plans that address every abnormal finding, not just the main diagnosis.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Electrolyte clues","pairs":[["Sodium 126 with confusion","Hyponatremia"],["Potassium 6.4 with peaked T waves","Hyperkalemia"],["Potassium 2.9 after diuretics","Hypokalemia"],["High BUN-to-creatinine ratio","Dehydration / prerenal state"],["Calcium 12.8 with confusion and constipation","Hypercalcemia"],["Low glucose with sweating and confusion","Hypoglycemia"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Diabetes: A1c ≥6.5%, fasting ≥126, OGTT 2-hour ≥200 or random ≥200 with symptoms; DKA = hyperglycemia, ketones, acidosis—IV fluids, insulin, potassium.','Hypothyroid: high TSH, levothyroxine; hyperthyroid/Graves: low TSH, exophthalmos; Cushing (excess cortisol) and Addison’s (deficiency, hyperpigmentation).','Kidneys contain nephrons; cystitis vs pyelonephritis (fever, flank pain, CVA tenderness); stones (colicky flank-to-groin pain, CT); AKI pre/intra/postrenal; CKD eGFR <60 for 3 months; ectopic pregnancy and testicular torsion are emergencies.'],terms:[['Nephron','The microscopic filtering unit of the kidney.'],['Diabetic ketoacidosis','A diabetes emergency with high glucose, ketones and acidosis.'],['Ectopic pregnancy','A pregnancy implanted outside the uterus.']],mcqs:[q('Kidneys contain millions of tiny structures called what?','Nephrons','Cysts','Ureters','Calyx','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which A1c indicates diabetes?','6.5% or higher','5.0%','5.7%','4.5%','5.7–6.4% is prediabetes.'),q('High TSH with low free T4 indicates:','Hypothyroidism','Hyperthyroidism','Diabetes','Cushing syndrome','Underactive thyroid.'),q('Fever, flank pain and CVA tenderness suggest:','Pyelonephritis','Simple cystitis','BPH','Kidney stone without infection','Kidney infection.'),q('Which test confirms a kidney stone?','Noncontrast CT','Lipid panel','Spirometry','EEG','Gold standard.'),q('Absent cremasteric reflex with sudden testicular pain suggests:','Testicular torsion','Epididymitis only','BPH','Prostate cancer','Surgical emergency.'),q('The most commonly reported bacterial STI in the United States is:','Chlamydia','Syphilis','Gonorrhea','HPV','Often asymptomatic.'),q('Hyperpigmentation, low blood pressure and high potassium suggest:','Addison’s disease','Cushing syndrome','Graves’ disease','Type 2 diabetes','Adrenal insufficiency.')],frq:{prompt:'A 26-year-old woman presents with 6 hours of right lower abdominal pain and light vaginal bleeding. Her last period was 7 weeks ago, her urine hCG is positive and she is dizzy with a heart rate of 118. Present your differential, diagnostic steps, leading diagnosis and treatment plan.',rubric:[['Differential',['ectopic','miscarriage','appendicitis','ovarian torsion','ovarian cyst','pid']],['Diagnostic steps',['quantitative hcg','transvaginal ultrasound','cbc','blood type','rh','vital signs']],['Diagnosis and urgency',['ectopic pregnancy','rupture','bleeding','shock','emergency']],['Treatment',['iv','fluids','blood','surgery','laparoscopy','methotrexate','rh immune globulin','follow-up','support']]],model:'With pelvic pain, vaginal bleeding and a positive pregnancy test, my differential includes ectopic pregnancy, threatened or incomplete miscarriage, a ruptured ovarian cyst, ovarian torsion, appendicitis and pelvic inflammatory disease, but ectopic pregnancy is the can’t-miss diagnosis. Her dizziness and heart rate of 118 suggest blood loss. I would place two large IV lines, start fluids, obtain a CBC, blood type and Rh status, a crossmatch and a quantitative hCG, and perform an urgent transvaginal ultrasound looking for an intrauterine pregnancy, an adnexal mass and free fluid. If the ultrasound shows no intrauterine pregnancy, an adnexal mass and free fluid, the leading diagnosis is a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, which is a surgical emergency. Treatment is resuscitation with fluids and blood as needed and emergency surgery, usually laparoscopy, to remove the ectopic pregnancy and stop the bleeding; methotrexate is reserved for stable, early, unruptured cases. If she is Rh-negative, she receives Rh immune globulin. Follow-up includes hCG monitoring if needed, emotional support and counseling about future pregnancies and contraception.'},sources:[RULES,ADA_DX,NIDDK_THY,MEDLINE_KIDNEY,MEDLINE_UTI,MEDLINE_ECTOPIC,CDC_STI]};

export const A9={title:'Nervous system, senses and mental disorders',hook:'A sudden facial droop, a fever with a stiff neck, a painful red eye with halos around lights, a teenager who has stopped eating and sleeping and says life isn’t worth living—each is time-sensitive, and each depends on careful assessment. Nervous system and senses (8%) and mental disorders (6%) together make up 14% of the test.',objectives:['Recognize and plan treatment for stroke, meningitis, seizures and headaches.','Distinguish Bell’s palsy from stroke and recognize other neurologic disorders.','Describe common eye and ear disorders, including emergencies.','Diagnose depression, anxiety, bipolar disorder and schizophrenia, and assess suicide risk.'],sections:[
['Stroke and transient ischemic attack',[
'A **stroke** causes sudden neurologic deficits: **face drooping, arm or leg weakness, speech difficulty, vision loss, imbalance**—remember **BE FAST**. **Ischemic** strokes (most) are caused by clots; **hemorrhagic** strokes by bleeding. The first test is an emergency **noncontrast head CT** to rule out bleeding. Eligible ischemic stroke patients receive **thrombolysis** (alteplase or tenecteplase) within **4.5 hours** of when they were **last known well**, and those with a large-vessel blockage may have **mechanical thrombectomy**—in selected patients up to 24 hours. Check **glucose**, because low blood sugar can mimic stroke.',
'A **transient ischemic attack (TIA)** causes stroke symptoms that resolve, usually within an hour, without permanent damage; it is a warning sign that requires urgent evaluation. Secondary prevention includes antiplatelet therapy, statins, blood pressure control, anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, diabetes control, smoking cessation and sometimes carotid surgery.',
label('brain-lateral','Brain regions and deficits',[['frontal','Frontal lobe','Personality, judgment and planning.'],['motor','Motor cortex','Weakness on the opposite side when damaged.'],['broca','Broca’s area','Expressive aphasia.'],['wernicke','Wernicke’s area','Receptive aphasia.'],['occipital','Occipital lobe','Vision loss.'],['cerebellum','Cerebellum','Ataxia and imbalance.']]),
order('Acute stroke steps',['Recognize BE FAST signs; call EMS','Note the time last known well','Check glucose','Emergency noncontrast head CT','Thrombolysis within 4.5 hours if eligible','Thrombectomy for large-vessel occlusion in selected patients'])]],
['Meningitis, seizures and headache',[
'**Meningitis** is inflammation of the membranes around the brain and spinal cord. **Bacterial** meningitis causes **fever, severe headache, stiff neck**, photophobia and confusion, sometimes with a **petechial rash** (meningococcus). **Kernig’s** and **Brudzinski’s** signs may be present. Diagnosis is by **lumbar puncture** (after CT if indicated), but **empiric IV antibiotics** must not be delayed. Close contacts of meningococcal cases receive preventive antibiotics, and vaccines prevent several types.',
'A **seizure** is abnormal electrical brain activity. **Epilepsy** means recurrent unprovoked seizures. A seizure lasting **5 minutes or more**, or repeated seizures without recovery, is **status epilepticus**, an emergency treated with **benzodiazepines**. Evaluate first seizures with glucose, electrolytes, toxicology, imaging and **EEG**. **Migraine** causes throbbing, often one-sided headache with nausea and sensitivity to light and sound, sometimes with aura; **tension** headaches are band-like; **cluster** headaches cause severe pain around one eye. A **sudden “thunderclap,” worst-ever headache** suggests **subarachnoid hemorrhage**—an emergency.',
match('Neurologic clues',[['Fever, stiff neck, photophobia','Meningitis'],['Seizure lasting 5 minutes or more','Status epilepticus'],['Worst headache of life, sudden onset','Subarachnoid hemorrhage'],['Throbbing one-sided headache with aura','Migraine'],['Whole half of face weak, including forehead','Bell’s palsy'],['Resting tremor and bradykinesia','Parkinson’s disease']])]],
['Other neurologic disorders',[
'**Bell’s palsy** is sudden weakness of one whole side of the face—including the **forehead**—from facial nerve inflammation; a stroke usually **spares the forehead**. It is treated with corticosteroids and eye protection, and most people recover. **Parkinson’s disease** causes tremor, rigidity, slow movement and postural instability and is treated with carbidopa/levodopa. **Multiple sclerosis** is autoimmune demyelination causing episodes of vision loss (optic neuritis), numbness and weakness; MRI shows lesions.',
'**Alzheimer’s disease**, the most common dementia, causes progressive memory loss and functional decline. **Delirium** is an acute, fluctuating confusion—often from infection, medications or dehydration in older adults—and is a medical emergency, unlike dementia. **Concussion** (mild traumatic brain injury) causes headache, confusion and memory problems after a blow to the head; rest followed by graded return to activity is the plan, and red flags such as worsening headache, repeated vomiting or seizures require imaging.',
sort('Delirium or dementia?',['Delirium','Dementia'],[['Sudden onset over hours to days',0],['Gradual decline over years',1],['Attention fluctuates during the day',0],['Often caused by infection or medicine',0],['Alzheimer’s disease',1],['Usually reversible when the cause is treated',0]])]],
['Eyes and ears',[
'**Open-angle glaucoma** causes gradual, painless loss of **peripheral vision** from optic nerve damage, often with high eye pressure; it is treated with eye drops. **Acute angle-closure glaucoma** is an emergency: a **painful red eye**, blurred vision, **halos around lights**, headache and vomiting. **Cataracts** cloud the lens and cause blurry, glare-sensitive vision, treated with surgery. **Macular degeneration** destroys **central vision**. **Diabetic retinopathy** requires yearly eye exams. **Conjunctivitis** (pink eye) is usually viral; bacterial cases have thick discharge.',
'**Acute otitis media** (middle-ear infection), common in young children, causes ear pain, fever and a **bulging, red tympanic membrane**; some cases are watched, others treated with amoxicillin. **Otitis externa** (swimmer’s ear) causes pain when the outer ear is pulled. **Hearing loss** may be conductive (wax, fluid) or sensorineural (inner ear, aging, noise). **Vertigo** may come from inner-ear problems such as BPPV or from the brain.',
label('eye','The eye',[['cornea','Cornea','Clear front surface.'],['lens','Lens','Clouds in cataracts.'],['retina','Retina','Damaged in diabetic retinopathy.'],['macula','Macula','Central vision; lost in macular degeneration.'],['optic','Optic nerve','Damaged in glaucoma.'],['iris','Iris','Pushed forward in angle-closure glaucoma.']])]],
['Mental disorders',[
'**Major depressive disorder** requires at least **two weeks** with **five or more** symptoms, including **depressed mood or loss of interest (anhedonia)**, plus changes in sleep, appetite, energy, concentration, feelings of worthlessness, psychomotor changes or thoughts of death. The **PHQ-9** screens and tracks severity. Treatment includes psychotherapy (such as CBT) and antidepressants such as SSRIs. **Generalized anxiety disorder** involves excessive worry most days for **at least six months** with restlessness, fatigue and poor sleep; the **GAD-7** screens for it. **Panic disorder** causes sudden attacks of fear with chest pain and palpitations—after ruling out medical causes.',
'**Bipolar I disorder** includes at least one **manic episode** (lasting a week or more, or any duration if hospitalized) with decreased need for sleep, grandiosity, racing thoughts and risky behavior; mood stabilizers such as lithium are used. **Schizophrenia** includes **hallucinations**, **delusions**, disorganized speech and negative symptoms for at least six months, treated with antipsychotics. **Substance use disorders**, **eating disorders** (anorexia, bulimia) and **PTSD** are also common. **Suicide risk** must be asked about directly; asking does not increase risk. Patients at risk need safety planning, removal of means and urgent care; the **988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline** is available by call or text.',
sort('Which disorder?',['Major depression','Generalized anxiety','Bipolar I','Schizophrenia'],[['Two weeks of anhedonia, poor sleep and hopelessness',0],['Six months of excessive worry about many things',1],['Week of no sleep, grandiosity and racing thoughts',2],['Hearing voices and fixed false beliefs for months',3],['PHQ-9 used to screen',0],['GAD-7 used to screen',1]])]],
['Practice case: sudden weakness',[
kase('Case: sudden weakness',{a:{text:'A 67-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who stopped her anticoagulant woke at 6:00 a.m. normal; at 7:30 her husband noticed right arm weakness and slurred speech. She arrives at 8:15. Glucose is 112. (Case invented for practice.) What is the next step?',choices:[['Emergency noncontrast head CT','b','Correct. Rule out hemorrhage before thrombolysis.',true],['Lumbar puncture','b','CT comes first for suspected stroke.',false]]},b:{text:'CT shows no bleeding. She was last seen normal at 6:00 a.m., and symptoms were noticed at 7:30. Which time starts the treatment clock?',choices:[['6:00 a.m., the last time she was known to be normal','c','Correct. Thrombolysis timing uses last known well, so her window closes at 10:30 a.m.',true],['8:15 a.m., when she arrived at the hospital','c','Arrival time is not the start time.',false]]},c:{text:'Within 4.5 hours, she receives thrombolysis, CT angiography for a large-vessel clot, then anticoagulation planning for AFib, a statin, BP control and stroke rehabilitation.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Stroke: BE FAST, glucose, noncontrast head CT, thrombolysis within 4.5 hours of last known well, thrombectomy for selected large-vessel occlusions; TIA is a warning.','Meningitis (fever, stiff neck; LP and immediate antibiotics); status epilepticus ≥5 minutes; thunderclap headache suggests subarachnoid hemorrhage; Bell’s palsy involves the forehead; delirium is acute and reversible.','Eyes: open-angle glaucoma (peripheral loss), angle-closure (painful red eye, halos—emergency), cataract, macular degeneration; depression ≥2 weeks with ≥5 symptoms, GAD ≥6 months, mania in bipolar I, psychosis in schizophrenia; ask about suicide and share 988.'],terms:[['Thrombolysis','Clot-dissolving treatment for ischemic stroke.'],['Status epilepticus','A seizure lasting 5 minutes or more.'],['Anhedonia','Loss of interest or pleasure in activities.']],mcqs:[q('What is the first imaging test for suspected acute stroke?','Noncontrast head CT','Chest X-ray','Abdominal ultrasound','Spirometry','Rule out hemorrhage.'),q('Thrombolysis for ischemic stroke is generally given within:','4.5 hours of last known well','24 hours of arrival','1 week','10 minutes only','Time is brain.'),q('Fever, stiff neck and photophobia suggest:','Meningitis','Migraine only','Bell’s palsy','Cataract','Do not delay antibiotics.'),q('Facial weakness that includes the forehead suggests:','Bell’s palsy','Stroke','Parkinson’s disease','Migraine','Stroke usually spares the forehead.'),q('A painful red eye with halos around lights suggests:','Acute angle-closure glaucoma','Cataract','Open-angle glaucoma','Viral conjunctivitis only','Emergency.'),q('Major depression requires symptoms for at least:','2 weeks','2 days','6 months','1 year','With five or more symptoms.'),q('A week of decreased need for sleep, grandiosity and racing thoughts suggests:','A manic episode','Generalized anxiety','Dementia','Delirium','Bipolar I.'),q('Which statement about asking about suicide is true?','Asking directly does not increase risk','Asking puts the idea in their head','Only psychiatrists may ask','It should be avoided','Ask clearly and get help.')],frq:{prompt:'A 17-year-old has 3 weeks of sadness, loss of interest in sports, poor sleep, low appetite and falling grades, and says he sometimes thinks his family would be better off without him. Present his patient information, diagnosis and a complete treatment and safety plan.',rubric:[['Patient information',['17','adolescent','sadness','anhedonia','sleep','appetite','grades','3 weeks']],['Diagnosis',['major depressive disorder','2 weeks','five','phq-9','rule out','thyroid','substance']],['Safety',['suicide','ask directly','plan','means','safety plan','988','urgent','guardian']],['Treatment',['psychotherapy','cbt','ssri','fluoxetine','boxed warning','follow-up','school','family']]],model:'The patient is a 17-year-old male with three weeks of persistent sadness, anhedonia—loss of interest in sports—insomnia, decreased appetite, poor concentration reflected in falling grades and passive suicidal thoughts that his family would be better off without him. He meets criteria for major depressive disorder: at least two weeks of five or more symptoms, including depressed mood and anhedonia. I would use the PHQ-9 to measure severity and rule out medical and other causes with a history and exam, thyroid testing and screening for substance use and bipolar symptoms. Safety comes first: I would ask directly whether he has thoughts of killing himself, a plan, intent, access to means such as firearms or medications and any past attempts. With his guardian involved, we would create a safety plan, remove or lock up means, share the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline and arrange an urgent mental health evaluation—or emergency care if risk is high. Treatment includes psychotherapy such as cognitive behavioral therapy and, for moderate to severe depression, an SSRI such as fluoxetine, with education about the boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in young people and close follow-up within one to two weeks. I would coordinate with his family and school for support and monitor his PHQ-9 over time.'},sources:[RULES,NINDS_STROKE,MEDLINE_MENING,NEI_GLAUCOMA,NIMH_DEP,LIFELINE]};

export const A10={title:'Skin and musculoskeletal disorders, and a full practice case',hook:'A changing mole, a hot red leg, a swollen big toe at 2 a.m., an older woman who breaks her wrist in a minor fall—skin and bone problems are common and often visible. Integumentary (4%) and musculoskeletal (6%) disorders complete the body systems. This lesson ends with a full practice case, from documents to a five-minute presentation.',objectives:['Recognize common skin infections, inflammatory conditions and skin cancers, including the ABCDEs of melanoma.','Distinguish gout, osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, and diagnose osteoporosis.','Identify low back pain red flags, including cauda equina syndrome.','Apply the full Round Two process to a practice case.'],sections:[
['Skin infections and inflammatory conditions',[
'**Cellulitis** is a bacterial infection of the deeper skin (usually streptococci or staphylococci) causing a **spreading, warm, red, tender** area, often on the leg, sometimes with fever. It is treated with antibiotics, and the border is marked to track spread. An **abscess** is a pocket of pus treated with **incision and drainage**; MRSA is a common cause. **Impetigo** causes honey-crusted sores in children. **Herpes zoster (shingles)** causes painful blisters in a **dermatome** on one side of the body; antivirals help, and the recombinant vaccine prevents it in adults 50 and older.',
'**Atopic dermatitis (eczema)** causes itchy, dry patches in skin folds, often with allergies and asthma; it is treated with moisturizers and topical steroids. **Psoriasis** causes well-defined, **silvery-scaled plaques** on the elbows, knees and scalp and is linked to psoriatic arthritis and heart disease. **Contact dermatitis** follows exposure to irritants or allergens such as poison ivy or nickel. **Urticaria** (hives) are itchy welts from allergic reactions.',
match('Skin condition clues',[['Spreading warm red leg with fever','Cellulitis'],['Fluctuant painful lump with pus','Abscess'],['Honey-colored crusts in a child','Impetigo'],['Painful blisters in one dermatome','Shingles'],['Silvery-scaled plaques on elbows','Psoriasis'],['Itchy dry patches in skin folds','Atopic dermatitis']])]],
['Skin cancer',[
'**Basal cell carcinoma** is the **most common** skin cancer—a pearly bump with rolled edges, often on sun-exposed skin; it rarely spreads. **Squamous cell carcinoma** appears as a scaly, crusted lesion and can spread. **Melanoma** is less common but most deadly; check moles with the **ABCDEs**: **A**symmetry, **B**order irregularity, **C**olor variation, **D**iameter larger than about **6 mm**, and **E**volving (changing size, shape or color). Suspicious lesions need a **biopsy**.',
'Risk factors include **UV exposure**, sunburns (especially in childhood), tanning beds, fair skin, many moles and family history. Prevention includes broad-spectrum sunscreen of SPF 30 or higher, protective clothing, shade and avoiding tanning beds. Treatment ranges from excision to immunotherapy for advanced melanoma.',
flip('ABCDEs of melanoma',[['A','Asymmetry—one half does not match the other'],['B','Border—irregular, ragged or blurred'],['C','Color—several shades or uneven color'],['D','Diameter—larger than about 6 mm'],['E','Evolving—changing size, shape or color'],['Next step','Biopsy a suspicious lesion']]),
label('skin','Skin layers',[['epidermis','Epidermis','Where most skin cancers start.'],['dermis','Dermis','Cellulitis spreads here.'],['hypodermis','Subcutaneous layer','Abscesses can form here.'],['vessel','Blood vessel','Melanoma can spread through vessels.'],['follicle','Hair follicle','Infected in folliculitis.']])]],
['Joints and bones',[
'**Gout** results from **uric acid crystals** in a joint: sudden, intensely painful, red, swollen joint—classically the **big toe (first MTP joint)**—often at night after alcohol, red meat or dehydration. Joint fluid shows **needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals**. Flares are treated with NSAIDs, colchicine or steroids; **allopurinol** lowers uric acid long term. **Septic arthritis** (a hot, swollen joint with fever) is an emergency diagnosed by joint aspiration.',
'**Osteoarthritis** is wear-and-tear cartilage loss with pain on use and brief stiffness; **rheumatoid arthritis** is autoimmune, symmetric and involves the small joints of the hands with prolonged morning stiffness, **rheumatoid factor** and **anti-CCP** antibodies, treated with disease-modifying drugs such as methotrexate. **Osteoporosis** is diagnosed by a **DEXA scan T-score of −2.5 or lower** (or a fragility fracture); treatment includes calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, fall prevention and **bisphosphonates**. Women 65 and older should be screened.',
sort('Gout, OA or RA?',['Gout','Osteoarthritis','Rheumatoid arthritis'],[['Sudden red, hot big toe at night',0],['Needle-shaped uric acid crystals',0],['Knee pain worse with walking, brief stiffness',1],['Symmetric hand joints with long morning stiffness',2],['Positive anti-CCP antibodies',2],['Cartilage loss and bone spurs',1]])]],
['Back pain and fractures',[
'Most **low back pain** is mechanical and improves within weeks with activity, heat, NSAIDs and physical therapy; imaging is not needed early. **Red flags** require urgent evaluation: **cauda equina syndrome** (saddle numbness, urinary retention or incontinence, bilateral leg weakness—a surgical emergency), fever or IV drug use (infection), a history of cancer or unexplained weight loss (metastasis), significant trauma or osteoporosis (fracture) and progressive neurologic deficits. A herniated disc can cause **sciatica**—pain radiating below the knee.',
'**Fractures** are diagnosed with X-rays; **open fractures** need antibiotics and surgery. **Hip fractures** in older adults cause a shortened, externally rotated leg and need surgery promptly. **Compartment syndrome**—severe pain out of proportion, especially with passive stretch, after a fracture or crush injury—is an emergency requiring fasciotomy. Sprains and strains are graded by severity.',
sort('Red flag or routine back pain?',['Red flag—urgent evaluation','Routine mechanical back pain'],[['Saddle numbness and urinary retention',0],['Back pain after lifting a box, no other symptoms',1],['Back pain with fever in a person who injects drugs',0],['History of breast cancer and new night pain',0],['Stiffness that improves with walking',1],['Progressive weakness in both legs',0]])]],
['Full practice case',[
'Case file (invented for practice): **Day 1**—a **58-year-old man** with type 2 diabetes, hypertension and a 30 pack-year smoking history comes to urgent care with **3 days of left-leg redness and swelling**. He works as a landscaper. Temp **101.4 °F**, HR 102, BP 136/84. The left shin has a **warm, tender, spreading red area** with a small cut from a garden tool; there is no fluctuance. Glucose 248 mg/dL, WBC 14,800. **Day 2**—the redness has spread beyond the marked border despite oral antibiotics, and his temperature is 102 °F. **Day 3**—he is admitted; ultrasound shows **no abscess** and **no DVT**; blood cultures grow *Streptococcus pyogenes*.',
'**Diagnosis:** **cellulitis** of the left lower leg that **failed outpatient therapy**, complicated by hyperglycemia, in a patient with diabetes. DVT and abscess were ruled out by ultrasound; necrotizing fasciitis was considered but there was no severe pain out of proportion, crepitus or rapid deterioration. **Treatment plan:** IV antibiotics (adjusted to culture results), elevation, marking the border, pain control, **glucose management** with insulin while hospitalized, wound care for the cut, a tetanus booster if not up to date, transition to oral antibiotics when improving, follow-up in 2–3 days, education on foot and skin checks, protective footwear and gloves at work, smoking cessation and diabetes control to prevent recurrence.',
order('Present this case',['Introduce the patient: 58-year-old landscaper with diabetes and hypertension','Chief complaint: 3 days of left-leg redness and swelling','Clinical course: Day 1 urgent care, Day 2 spread on oral antibiotics, Day 3 admission','Objective data: fever, WBC 14,800, glucose 248, ultrasound and cultures','Diagnosis: cellulitis failing outpatient therapy; DVT and abscess ruled out','Treatment: IV antibiotics, elevation, glucose control, wound care, tetanus, follow-up, prevention','Summary of key points']),
kase('Presentation decisions',{a:{text:'Your partner wants to spend 3 minutes on the full timeline. What is the risk?',choices:[['Too little time remains for diagnosis, treatment and summary—split time evenly','b','Correct. Diagnosis and treatment are worth 40 points.',true],['No risk; the timeline is most important','b','Balance the content to the rating sheet.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you show the judges your diagnosis is accurate?',choices:[['State the diagnosis and the evidence, and name the alternatives ruled out (DVT, abscess)','c','Correct. Evidence and differential strengthen accuracy.',true],['Say “we think it’s an infection”','c','Be specific and support it.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish with a one-sentence summary: cellulitis that failed oral therapy in a patient with diabetes, treated with IV antibiotics, glucose control and prevention education.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Cellulitis (spreading warm red skin; antibiotics), abscess (incision and drainage), shingles (dermatome), eczema and psoriasis; basal cell is most common, melanoma deadliest—use the ABCDEs and biopsy.','Gout (first MTP, needle-shaped crystals, allopurinol long term), OA vs RA (symmetric, anti-CCP), osteoporosis (DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5, bisphosphonates); back-pain red flags such as cauda equina syndrome.','In Round Two, present patient information, a chronological course, a supported diagnosis with ruled-out alternatives and a complete plan, then summarize with equal team roles.'],terms:[['Cellulitis','A bacterial infection of the deeper skin and tissue.'],['Cauda equina syndrome','Compression of lumbar and sacral nerve roots causing saddle numbness and bladder problems.'],['T-score','A bone density comparison with a healthy young adult; −2.5 or lower indicates osteoporosis.']],mcqs:[q('In the ABCDEs of melanoma, D stands for:','Diameter larger than about 6 mm','Dark color','Dry skin','Dermatome','Plus Evolving.'),q('The most common skin cancer is:','Basal cell carcinoma','Melanoma','Squamous cell carcinoma','Kaposi sarcoma','Rarely spreads.'),q('Painful blisters in a band on one side of the chest suggest:','Shingles','Psoriasis','Impetigo','Eczema','Dermatomal.'),q('Needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals indicate:','Gout','Pseudogout','Rheumatoid arthritis','Osteoarthritis','Uric acid.'),q('Osteoporosis is diagnosed by a DEXA T-score of:','−2.5 or lower','+2.5 or higher','0','−1.0 to +1.0','Or a fragility fracture.'),q('Saddle numbness and urinary retention with back pain suggest:','Cauda equina syndrome','Muscle strain','Osteoarthritis','Sciatica only','Surgical emergency.'),q('An abscess is primarily treated with:','Incision and drainage','Topical steroids','Sunscreen','Bisphosphonates','Plus antibiotics in some cases.'),q('Symmetric small-joint arthritis with long morning stiffness suggests:','Rheumatoid arthritis','Osteoarthritis','Gout','Septic arthritis','Autoimmune.')],frq:{prompt:'Using the practice case of the 58-year-old landscaper, deliver a written version of your five-minute presentation: patient information, clinical course, diagnosis with supporting evidence and ruled-out alternatives, a complete treatment plan and a summary.',rubric:[['Patient information',['58','landscaper','diabetes','hypertension','smoking','chief complaint','3 days']],['Clinical course',['day 1','day 2','day 3','spread','oral antibiotics','admitted','chronological']],['Diagnosis and evidence',['cellulitis','fever','wbc','ultrasound','no abscess','no dvt','culture','streptococcus','necrotizing']],['Treatment and summary',['iv antibiotics','elevation','glucose','insulin','wound','tetanus','follow-up','education','smoking','summary']]],model:'Our patient is a 58-year-old male landscaper with type 2 diabetes, hypertension and a 30 pack-year smoking history whose chief complaint was three days of left-leg redness and swelling. On day 1 at urgent care he had a temperature of 101.4 °F, a heart rate of 102, a warm, tender, spreading red area on the left shin near a small cut from a garden tool and no fluctuance; his white count was 14,800 and glucose 248. He started oral antibiotics. On day 2 the redness spread beyond the marked border and his fever rose to 102 °F. On day 3 he was admitted; ultrasound showed no abscess and no DVT, and blood cultures grew Streptococcus pyogenes. Our diagnosis is cellulitis of the left lower leg that failed outpatient therapy, complicated by hyperglycemia. We ruled out DVT and abscess by ultrasound and considered necrotizing fasciitis, but he lacked pain out of proportion, crepitus or rapid deterioration. Our treatment plan is IV antibiotics adjusted to culture results, leg elevation, marking the border, pain control, insulin to control glucose, wound care for the cut and a tetanus booster if needed, followed by oral antibiotics when he improves and follow-up in two to three days. To prevent recurrence we would teach daily skin and foot checks, gloves and protective footwear at work, smoking cessation and better diabetes control. In summary, this is streptococcal cellulitis that failed oral therapy in a patient with diabetes, treated with IV antibiotics, glucose management and prevention education.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_CELL,AAD_MELANOMA,NIAMS_GOUT,NIAMS_OSTEO]};
